    "result_text": "All tests pass. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe telephone is now stored and compared in E.164 form. Two files changed:\n\n1. **`NormalizeOwnerTelephone.java`** \u2014 the single normalize step (a one-method class, so zero surrounding complexity). It now: trims and strips all non-digits; if the input has a leading `+` it keeps the country code as-is; otherwise it drops a single leading `0` from the national digits and prepends `61`; requires 8\u201315 digits after the `+`, throwing `MissingOwnerFieldsException` (\u2192 400) otherwise; and stores `\"+\" + digits`. So `0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678`, `+64 21 123 456` \u2192 `+6421123456`, and `12` \u2192 400.\n\n2. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 the entity's telephone bean-validation constraint was hard-coded to exactly 10 digits (`@Digits` + `@Pattern ^[0-9]{10}$`), which rejected E.164 values with a 500 on save. Replaced with `@Pattern(\"^\\\\+[0-9]{8,15}$\")` and removed the now-unused `@Digits` import.\n\nThe duplicate-telephone check (`RejectDuplicateOwnerTelephone`) needed no change: it already compares stored `telephone` values, which are now the E.164 strings, and it runs after normalization in the POST pipeline.",
